            <div xml:id="d199">

                <pb facs="0289" n="185" sameAs="frus:frus1932v03/pg185"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94/3872: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at Shanghai (Cunningham) to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Shanghai</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1932-02-02T19:00:00+08:00">February 2, 1932—7
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received February 2—7:55 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>My telegram of January 31, 11 p.m. At 2:15 this afternoon I received oral
                    communication from Japanese Consulate General that instructions had been
                    received from Tokyo that the proposal made at the meeting on Sunday, January
                    31st, could not be accepted. Truce <pb facs="0290" n="186" xml:id="pg186"/>
                    appears to be broken as heavy firing began shortly after 2 o’clock. Japanese
                    Consul General denied to me over telephone that truce broken stating that
                    Japanese had been attacked and were only replying. Japanese aeroplanes have been
                    in the air since 2 o’clock.</p>
                <p>Repeated to the Legation and Nanking.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Cunningham</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
